===Conjunction===
{{en-con}}

# {{l|en|however|However}}, {{l|en|although}}, {{l|en|nevertheless}}, {{l|en|on the other hand}} {{q|introducing a clause contrary to prior belief or in contrast with the preceding clause or sentence}}.
#: {{ux|en|She is very old '''but''' still attractive.}}
#: {{ux|en|You told me I could do that, '''but''' she said that I could not.}}
# {{l|en|on the contrary|On the contrary}}, {{l|en|rather}} {{q|as a regular {{l|en|adversative}} conjunction, introducing a word or clause in {{l|en|contrast}} or {{l|en|contradiction}} with the preceding negative clause or sentence}}.
#: {{ux|en|I am not rich '''but''' [I am] poor.&emsp; Not John '''but''' Peter went there.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} {{ng|Used at the beginning of a sentence to express [[opposition]] to a [[remark]].}}
#: {{ux|en|'''But''' I never said you could do that!}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|volume=IV|book=X|passage=In reality, I apprehend every amorous widow on the stage would run the hazard of being condemned as a servile imitation of Dido, '''but''' that happily very few of our play-house critics understand enough of Latin to read Virgil.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-19|author=[http://www.theguardian.com/profile/iansample Ian Sample]|volume=189|issue=6|page=34|magazine={{w|The Guardian Weekly}}|title=[http://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2013/jul/08/irregular-bedtimes-affect-childrens-brains Irregular bedtimes may affect children's brains]|passage=Irregular bedtimes may disrupt healthy brain development in young children, according to a study of intelligence and sleeping habits.<br>Going to bed at a different time each night affected girls more than boys, '''but''' both fared worse on mental tasks than children who had a set bedtime, researchers found.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=55| magazine={{w|The Economist}}| title=[http://www.economist.com/news/international/21580191-edward-snowdens-odyssey-leaves-america-nonplussed-and-its-allies-dismayed-russia-china Travels and travails]| passage=Even without hovering drones, a lurking assassin, a thumping score and a denouement, the real-life story of Edward Snowden, a rogue spy on the run, could be straight out of the cinema. '''But''', as with Hollywood, the subplots and exotic locations may distract from the real message: America’s discomfort and its foes’ glee.}}
# [[except|Except]] that (introducing a subordinate clause which qualifies a negative statement); also, with omission of the subject of the subordinate clause, acting as a negative relative, "except one that", "except such that".
#: {{ux|en|I cannot '''but''' feel offended.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|IV|iii|text=And [[w:Saint Crispin's Day|Crispin Crispian]] shall ne’er go by,<br>From this day to the ending of the world,<br>'''''But''' we in it shall be rememberèd—}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=15|book=II|passage=There is no reason '''but''' hath another contrary unto it, saith the wisest party of Philosophers.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|III|iv|passage=And '''but''' my noble Moor is true of mind<br>{{...}} it were enough to put him to ill thinking.}}
#* {{RQ:Keats Lamia|poem=Lamia|part=II|page=43|passage=A deadly silence step by step increased, <br> Until it seem'd a horrid presence there, <br> And not a man '''but''' felt the terror in his hair.}}
#* {{RQ:Thoreau Walden|chapter=Economy|passage=In those days, when my hands were much employed, I read but little, '''but''' the least scraps of paper which lay on the ground, my holder, or tablecloth, afforded me as much entertainment, in fact answered the same purpose as the Iliad.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} {{n-g|Used to link an interjection to the following remark as an intensifier.}}
#: {{ux|en|Wow! '''But''' that's amazing!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Nora Roberts|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=BiX8HdYyPY0C&pg=PT25|title=Irish Thoroughbred|page=25|publisher=Little, Brown|isbn=9781405523516
|passage="Jakers, '''but''' we worked." With a long breath she shut her eyes. "But it was too much for one woman and a half-grown girl {{...}}"}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Without it also being the case that; unless that (introducing a necessary concomitant).
#: {{ux|en|It never rains '''but''' it pours.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|II|VI|passage=No arboret with painted blossomes drest, / And smelling sweet, '''but''' there it might be found {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|II|ii|passage=For there is nothing either good or bad, '''but''' thinking makes it so}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Except with; unless with; without.
#* {{RQ:Fuller Holy Warre|chapter=Unseasonable Discords betwixt King Baldwine and His Mother; Her Strength in Yeelding to Her Sonne|page=84|passage=This man unable to manage his own happineſſe, grew ſo inſolent that he could not go, '''but''' either ſpurning his equals, or trampling on his inferiours.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Only; solely; merely.
#* {{RQ:Milton Of Reformation|passage=Observe '''but''' how their own principles combat one another.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Metamorphoses|passage=a formidable man '''but''' to his friends}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[until|Until]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|passage='Tshall not be long '''but''' I'll be here again.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|following a negated expression of improbability}} [[that|That]]. {{defdate|16th–19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1784|author={{w|Joshua Reynolds}}|editors={{w|John Ingamells}}; {{w|John Edgcumbe}}|title=The Letters of Sir Joshua Reynolds|publisher=Yale|year_published=2000|page=131
|passage=It is not impossible '''but''' next year I may have the honour of waiting on your Lordship at St. Asaph, If I go to Ireland I certainly will go that way.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1789|author={{w|John Moore (Scottish physician)|John Moore}}|title=Zeluco|publisher=Valancourt|year_published=2008|page=132
|passage=“I am convinced, if you were to press this matter earnestly upon her, she would consent.”<br>“It is not impossible '''but''' she might,” said Madame de Seidlits {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1813|journal=Journal of Natural Philosophy|month=July
|passage=It is not improbable '''but''' future observations will add Pliny's Well to the class of irregular reciprocators.}}

====Usage notes====
* It is generally considered colloquial to use ''but'' at the beginning of a sentence, with other conjunctions such as ''however'' or ''although'' being preferred in formal writing.
** {{ux|en|'''But''' this tool has its uses.}}
** {{ux|en|However, this tool has its uses.}}
** {{ux|en|Although this tool does have its uses.}}

